A||||9||formula2||Spark plugs||||5||
Z||000000||formula2||07-20-2001||11:43 AM||||Hi there..<BR>What is the best way to remove the plugs behind the engine.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||07-20-2001||03:52 PM||||Be sure to use a quality socket, if you round the plug your in for a long job.<P>"lefty loosey-righty tighty"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bruce||07-20-2001||05:28 PM||||Try to stand in the trunk space- you'll have better positioning to remove the back three plugs. When you replace the plugs, be sure to put a little anti-seize oil on the threads.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BENGAL4||07-20-2001||06:29 PM||||I have changed plugs on a 2.8 twice. The first time, I left the deck lid on. It took about 2 hours. It was very uncomfortable and very frustrating. The second time, I took the rear deck lid off and it took 15 minutes. Removing the lid is easy, just scribe marks around the edge of the hinges. You might need some help to put it back on, but be sure to line it up before you tighten the bolts. Good luck.<P><BR>Bill<BR>86GT<BR>3800 Series II S/C||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||07-20-2001||08:16 PM||||its a PITA on the V6, just did mine. I dont have half the room the stock body has to work in. I still think they should have put a bolt in panel in the firewall behind the passenger seat......but that would have been too ez. If i do another kit or engine replace, im cutting a 18'' square hole back there and bolting it in by welding nuts to the backside. Surprized no one else has never done it. EZ access to everything on the backside.||||reg||1
Z||000005||formula2||07-21-2001||10:10 AM||||Thanks<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>took the<BR>                      rear deck lid off and it took 15 minutes.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>my english is not up to date...<P>obviously no one speaks french here so could you explain" rear deck lid".I need to know...<P>Thank you..||||reg||9
Z||000006||Cheever2||07-21-2001||10:25 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> could you explain" rear deck lid".I need to know<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sure...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/decklid_4.jpg"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bruce||07-21-2001||01:28 PM||||Interesting looking rear deck lid.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cheever2||07-21-2001||03:57 PM||||Not mine.  "Borrowed" the pic from grinthock's "idea - comments" topic.<P>I guess "rear decklid" is redundant.  Generally, we only say 'decklid' when we mean the rear, as most people (my observation anyway) say "hood" for the front.  <P>Have no idea what terms AusFiero uses for these things.  He probably says "bow" and "stern".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  In some places, a deck is off the back door, and you better have a gun when you're in the Hood.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||88CoupeV6||07-21-2001||05:15 PM||||I have no prob. sitting in the trunk and reaching around the plenum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
